All four are done. Here's each one.

## 1. The `ParticipantsManager` crash

`_ParticipantCountWidget` asked GetIt for a `ParticipantsManager`, but nothing registers one — and that is deliberate: [account_registrations.dart](apps/mobile/lib/app/composition/account_registrations.dart) creates it inside the session factory, so the room owns its roster and disposes it with itself (audit Steps 29/31: only `ActiveBroadcastManager` goes in the account scope). The widget was reading a singleton that intentionally does not exist, which is why the crash appeared the moment the live shell built.

[participant_list_header_widget.dart](apps/mobile/lib/features/broadcast/widgets/participant_list_header_widget.dart) now reads the roster through the one registered live owner — `di<ActiveBroadcastManager>().session?.participants` — watches the snapshot so a session that appears after the first build is picked up, and draws nothing when there is no room. [participants_modal.dart](apps/mobile/lib/features/broadcast/widgets/participants_modal.dart) had the identical `di<ParticipantsManager>()` at line 24 and would have crashed as soon as you tapped Expand; it now resolves the same way and returns an empty sheet instead of throwing if the room closed underneath it. Both keep their reactivity via `watch(manager.totalCount)` etc., since the object is not a DI singleton.

## 2. Ending always shows the ended page

- [live_session_banner.dart](apps/mobile/lib/features/broadcast/widgets/live_session_banner.dart) — after `leaveOrEnd`, a host end navigates to `endedBroadcastRoute(summary.id)`. This is the gap: inside a room the live shell already watches `phase == ended` and routes to the ended page, but the banner lives on Home where no shell is mounted, so the host who ended from there just watched the banner vanish. A listener *leaving* still goes nowhere, because leaving is not ending.
- [live_broadcast_page.dart](apps/mobile/lib/features/broadcast/pages/live_broadcast_page.dart) and [broadcast_control_buttons.dart](apps/mobile/lib/features/broadcast/widgets/broadcast_control_buttons.dart) both end through the manager while the live shell is mounted, so the shell's existing phase-`ended` branch takes them to [ended_broadcast_page.dart](apps/mobile/lib/features/broadcast/pages/ended_broadcast_page.dart). I did not duplicate navigation inside the button — that would race the shell's own transition.

The ended page loads by id when the retained summary is gone, so it works after a host end, a server-forced end, or a process restart.

## 3. Root shell tests

The tests never referenced the account shell — they assert bottom bar vs side rail and branch switching, which is unchanged. What was actually broken was [root_shell.dart](apps/mobile/lib/app/layouts/root_shell.dart) still importing the deleted `authenticated_shell.dart` while its body already rendered plain `navigationShell`. I removed that import and corrected the doc sentence to say the tab content is unwrapped and the banner belongs to Home. I did not touch your revert beyond those two lines, and I did not re-add any chrome.

One caveat: `flutter test test/root_shell_test.dart` **cannot run in this environment** — it fails before any assertion with `Failed to load dynamic library 'objectbox.dll'`, the same ObjectBox host-library gap that blocks the editor page test. So the file is unchanged and unverified here; it needs the ObjectBox library installed to execute.

## 4. Drafts no longer kept after a successful start

Your flow is now the implementation:

- **Pressing start writes nothing.** [broadcast_editor_page.dart](apps/mobile/lib/features/broadcast/pages/broadcast_editor_page.dart) calls `manager.buildDraft()`, which builds the draft in memory only ([broadcast_editor_manager.dart](apps/mobile/lib/features/broadcast/manager/broadcast_editor_manager.dart) `buildDraft` / `saveDraft` split).
- **A successful start leaves no draft.** Nothing was written; if the form came from a saved draft, `_forgetStartedDraft()` still deletes that row once the room is entered.
- **A failed start keeps one, automatically.** Three failure points call `_keepFailedStart()`: a create/upload error, a go-live error, and — the case that matters for your earlier bug — an entry that was refused (`!entry.isEntered`). The saved draft carries `createdBroadcastId` and `creationStep`, so reopening it resumes at the phase that failed ("Finalize Broadcast") rather than creating a second broadcast. A failure to save must not mask the error the user is about to see, so that path logs and continues.
- **Leaving without starting still asks**, and only writes when you choose Save (`_confirmAndLeave` was already correct; it just needed to stop being pre-empted by the button).

One trade-off this makes explicit: because nothing is written before the network call, a process kill *during* creation loses the device copy — previously the draft survived that. That follows directly from "draft should not be saved unless the user said so or the start failed"; if you want crash-during-create covered without keeping a draft on success, the draft would have to be written and then deleted on success, which is what the old code did.

